@@ -104,6 +104,7 @@ The Privacy Center, Data management, Coach's "How Coach uses your data", Help an
 1. **The support email (the one open flow).** Contact support opens the system mail composer (or a `mailto:` link) addressed to the support inbox, with the app version, build number and iOS version in the body. The person must press Send; nothing is sent by the app on its own, and NOCTIS has no server that receives it. Whether Apple's questionnaire counts an email the person chooses to send as data "collected" (Customer Support, and the sender's email address) is an interpretation. Option A (draft above): not collected, because the app transmits nothing and the person's mail app sends the message. Option B: declare Customer Support. This report does not decide it. **Owner.**
 2. **Health: onboarding asks for three kinds, the app reads five.** Onboarding asks Apple Health for sleep, heart rate and HRV (`HealthDataKind.core`, used by `HealthScreen.requestedKinds`); "Connect Apple Health" elsewhere asks for all five (`HealthDataKind.allCases`), and Settings, Health data offers it for the two kinds onboarding did not ask for (resting heart rate and respiratory rate). `HealthKitProvider.authorizationStatus()` asks HealthKit about the three core kinds only, so a person who has answered onboarding's sheet is not treated as "not asked yet", and Home, Sleep, Recovery and Insights load; the status means "the system's question was asked", never "read access was allowed", because HealthKit does not say which kinds were declined. HealthKit throws "authorization not determined" for a kind it was never asked about, so resting heart rate and respiratory rate are read through `restingHeartRateIfAsked` and `respiratorySamplesIfAsked`, which turn that into no data; the core reads are not softened. Checked against real HealthKit on the iOS 27 simulator (Stage 1A rework, `NOCTIS_STAGE1A_IMPLEMENTATION_REPORT.md` section 13): with the three core kinds allowed, or declined, Home shows the empty state; with Health skipped, Home shows "Connect Apple Health". Not checked on a physical iPhone or with real Health data. Without resting heart rate Recovery is worked out from the other factors and can differ by a few points from the score with it; the usage string names all five kinds while onboarding's sheet lists three. **Owner**: confirm on a real iPhone; decide whether Settings' note should say that granting the two optional kinds refines Recovery, or whether onboarding should ask for all five, and whether the usage string should name only the kinds a given sheet asks for.
 3. **Device backups.** The default SwiftData store (profile, wake alarm, wind-down history) and the preferences, sound choices and flags are in iCloud and computer backups, with iOS's standard file protection (readable after first unlock). The Journal, Coach conversations, insights and cached summaries are excluded from backups and use complete protection. The headline "Your data is stored on this iPhone." is true of where NOCTIS keeps it; a backup is Apple's copy of the iPhone, not NOCTIS syncing. The inventory says so in "What may leave", but the owner decides whether the headline should say "backups aside", whether the profile's optional name should be excluded from backups, and whether the default store should move to complete protection. **Owner.**
+   **What a new iPhone gets (S1B-B05, proposed wording, owner to approve before it goes on the site or the listing; the alarm sentence corrected in the Stage 1B corrective pass, R-12):** "NOCTIS keeps your data on your iPhone and has no copy of it anywhere else. If you set up a new iPhone from an iCloud or computer backup, your profile, the wake alarm's settings, wind-down history and other settings come with it (the alarm itself is set up with the system again by NOCTIS when it opens, since a backup does not restore system alarms). Your Journal, Coach conversations, insights and summaries do not, and neither does a Sign in with Apple link. Export your data first if you want to keep them." This follows from the backup flags above (the Journal, Coach, insights and cached summaries are excluded; the sign-in item is this device only) and says nothing about how the data could be recovered from the device. Nothing in it is a forensic claim: deleting one kind of data now also compacts its database at the next launch (frees the space), and "Delete all NOCTIS data" removes the files themselves.
 4. **Subscription.** Purchases go through StoreKit and Apple's App Store. There is no receipt validation server, so the developer receives nothing from the app. If a server or an analytics event for purchases is ever added, Purchases must be declared and the manifest changed.
 5. **"Deleted" and free pages.** Deleting one kind of data (for example Coach history) removes its rows; the SQLite file is not vacuumed, so the bytes of deleted text can stay in the file's free pages until they are overwritten (the Coach store stayed near 111 KB after its rows were deleted in testing). "Delete all NOCTIS data" removes the store files themselves. The page says "deleted". **Owner**: accept, reword ("removed from NOCTIS"), or add a vacuum after a single-category delete.
 6. **The app switcher's picture.** iOS keeps a small picture of the app's last screen for the app switcher. It can show a recovery score or a Journal entry. NOCTIS does not hide its screens when the app goes to the background. The inventory says so under "Files iOS makes for the app". **Owner**: whether to blur the content when the app becomes inactive.
